1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:09,215
In 2012, a team of scientists slid the
mummy of an Egyptian pharaoh into a CT

2
00:00:09,215 --> 00:00:10,631
scanner in Cairo.

3
00:00:11,181 --> 00:00:16,383
For more than a century, his neck had
been hidden under thick layers of linen,

4
00:00:16,383 --> 00:00:20,117
wrapped by embalmers more
than three thousand years ago.

5
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:26,073
What the scan revealed under those bandages
was a deep cut across the front of his

6
00:00:26,073 --> 00:00:26,535
throat.

7
00:00:27,085 --> 00:00:31,007
The last great warrior pharaoh
of Egypt had been murdered.

8
00:00:31,557 --> 00:00:36,759
And we know a surprising amount about who
did it, because the trial of his killers

9
00:00:36,759 --> 00:00:39,804
was written down, and the
records still survive.

10
00:00:40,354 --> 00:00:46,049
This is the story of a royal conspiracy,
a trial that went wrong, and a cold case

11
00:00:46,049 --> 00:00:48,862
that took three thousand years to close.

12
00:00:49,412 --> 00:00:52,395
A queen who wanted her son on the throne.

13
00:00:52,945 --> 00:00:56,535
A plot that used messengers,
rebels, and magic.

14
00:01:01,585 --> 00:01:03,893
This is Ancient World Files.

15
00:01:09,442 --> 00:01:16,617
Our story begins around the year 1186
B.C.E., when a man named Ramesses came to the

16
00:01:16,617 --> 00:01:18,000
throne of Egypt.

17
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:24,181
He was the second king of a new royal family,
the Twentieth Dynasty, and he took the

18
00:01:24,181 --> 00:01:29,678
name of the most famous pharaoh of all,
Ramesses the Second, the great builder who

19
00:01:29,678 --> 00:01:31,689
had died a generation earlier.

20
00:01:32,239 --> 00:01:37,186
Ramesses the Third wanted to be
seen as a new Ramesses the Great.

21
00:01:37,736 --> 00:01:39,969
And for a while, he succeeded.

22
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:46,181
On the west bank of the Nile at Thebes,
he built a vast memorial temple, Medinet

23
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:48,375
Habu, which still stands today.

24
00:01:48,925 --> 00:01:52,255
Its walls tell the story
of his reign in stone.

25
00:01:52,805 --> 00:01:58,372
In his eighth year, he fought off the
invasion of the Sea Peoples, in battles on

26
00:01:58,372 --> 00:02:03,939
land and at the mouths of the Nile, at a
time when empires all around Egypt were

27
00:02:03,939 --> 00:02:04,704
collapsing.

28
00:02:05,254 --> 00:02:07,827
He fought Libyan invaders twice.

29
00:02:08,377 --> 00:02:14,010
He built temples, gave enormous gifts to
the gods, and sent expeditions abroad.

30
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,605
But behind the triumphant
reliefs, Egypt was under strain.

31
00:02:19,155 --> 00:02:21,935
The great victories had been expensive.

32
00:02:22,485 --> 00:02:28,400
The eastern Mediterranean world that Egypt
had traded with for centuries was falling

33
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:28,822
apart.

34
00:02:32,372 --> 00:02:37,138
In the twenty ninth year of his reign,
something happened that had never been

35
00:02:37,138 --> 00:02:38,128
recorded before.

36
00:02:38,678 --> 00:02:43,777
The craftsmen who built the royal tombs
in the Valley of the Kings went on strike.

37
00:02:44,327 --> 00:02:47,036
Their grain rations had not arrived.

38
00:02:47,586 --> 00:02:53,296
They put down their tools, marched out, and
sat down behind the temples, refusing to

39
00:02:53,296 --> 00:02:55,063
work until they were paid.

40
00:02:55,613 --> 00:02:59,831
It is the earliest well documented
labor strike in history.

41
00:03:03,981 --> 00:03:09,165
The great pharaoh's government could no
longer feed the men building his own tomb.

42
00:03:09,715 --> 00:03:13,039
By now, Ramesses the Third was an old man.

43
00:03:13,589 --> 00:03:15,766
He had ruled for three decades.

44
00:03:16,316 --> 00:03:17,717
He had many sons.

45
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:23,210
Several of them were buried as young
princes in the Valley of the Queens, in

46
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:28,544
beautifully painted tombs that show their
father leading them by the hand into the

47
00:03:28,544 --> 00:03:29,910
presence of the gods.

48
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:33,014
Some of his sons died before him.

49
00:03:33,564 --> 00:03:38,399
The heir he eventually chose was
a prince also named Ramesses.

50
00:03:38,949 --> 00:03:43,191
And the question of who would
succeed him was becoming urgent.

51
00:03:48,741 --> 00:03:54,001
To understand what happened next, we
need to step inside one of the most

52
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:58,458
misunderstood institutions in
ancient Egypt: the royal harem.

53
00:03:59,008 --> 00:04:03,340
The word conjures images of
a secluded chamber of wives.

54
00:04:03,890 --> 00:04:10,154
In reality, the Egyptian royal harem was
a large institution, with its own estates,

55
00:04:10,154 --> 00:04:13,173
workshops, servants, and administrators.

56
00:04:13,723 --> 00:04:19,348
It housed the king's wives, his mother,
his young children, and many women of the

57
00:04:19,348 --> 00:04:24,556
court, along with officials who managed
its property and guarded its doors.

58
00:04:25,106 --> 00:04:30,627
At Medinet Habu itself, in the rooms above
the great eastern gate, there are reliefs

59
00:04:30,627 --> 00:04:34,045
that show the king at leisure
among the royal women.

60
00:04:34,595 --> 00:04:39,903
They bring him fruit and flowers, and in
one scene he plays a board game with one of

61
00:04:39,903 --> 00:04:40,219
them.

62
00:04:40,769 --> 00:04:45,594
These were the people closest to the king,
the people who could reach him when no

63
00:04:45,594 --> 00:04:46,487
one else could.

64
00:04:47,037 --> 00:04:49,969
Some of these women had great influence.

65
00:04:50,519 --> 00:04:52,538
And some of them had sons.

66
00:04:53,088 --> 00:04:56,658
One of the king's wives
was a woman named Tiye.

67
00:04:57,208 --> 00:05:01,464
She was not the chief queen, and
her son was not the chosen heir.

68
00:05:02,014 --> 00:05:06,561
Her son's name, as it appears
in the records, was Pentawere.

69
00:05:07,111 --> 00:05:12,245
The crown prince was another son of the
king, the future Ramesses the Fourth.

70
00:05:12,795 --> 00:05:16,920
Tiye decided that her own son
should be pharaoh instead.

71
00:05:17,470 --> 00:05:19,642
We do not know exactly why.

72
00:05:20,192 --> 00:05:22,076
Perhaps it was ambition.

73
00:05:22,626 --> 00:05:27,959
Perhaps it was fear, because when a new
king took the throne, the mother of a rival

74
00:05:27,959 --> 00:05:31,878
prince could lose her position,
her wealth, and perhaps more.

75
00:05:36,028 --> 00:05:39,307
And to make that happen,
the king would have to die.

76
00:05:39,857 --> 00:05:43,040
To an Egyptian, this
was almost unthinkable.

77
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:46,143
The pharaoh was not just a ruler.

78
00:05:46,693 --> 00:05:52,498
He was the living guardian of maat, the
order of the universe, the balance of truth

79
00:05:52,498 --> 00:05:54,946
and justice that kept chaos at bay.

80
00:05:55,496 --> 00:06:01,315
Every morning, in temples across Egypt,
rituals were performed in his name to keep

81
00:06:01,315 --> 00:06:03,940
the sun rising and the Nile flooding.

82
00:06:04,489 --> 00:06:07,341
To kill the king was not simply murder.

83
00:06:07,891 --> 00:06:11,280
It was an attack on the
order of the world itself.

84
00:06:16,830 --> 00:06:22,674
Almost everything we know about the
conspiracy comes from a handful of papyri, the

85
00:06:22,674 --> 00:06:27,877
most important of which is now in the
Egyptian Museum in Turin, in Italy.

86
00:06:28,427 --> 00:06:34,686
It is known as the Judicial Papyrus of
Turin, and it is, in effect, the case file.

87
00:06:35,236 --> 00:06:40,154
It lists the accused, one by one, with
their titles, what they did, and what

88
00:06:40,154 --> 00:06:41,254
happened to them.

89
00:06:41,804 --> 00:06:47,658
At the center of the plot, alongside Tiye,
was a man named Pebekkamen, the chief of

90
00:06:47,658 --> 00:06:51,889
the chamber, one of the most
trusted servants in the palace.

91
00:06:52,439 --> 00:06:55,976
With him was a royal
butler named Mesedsure.

92
00:06:56,526 --> 00:07:01,610
The plot spread through the staff of
the harem itself, its overseers and its

93
00:07:01,610 --> 00:07:02,145
scribes.

94
00:07:02,695 --> 00:07:07,791
The conspirators had a problem: the
women of the harem were closely guarded.

95
00:07:08,341 --> 00:07:10,373
So they used messengers.

96
00:07:10,923 --> 00:07:16,284
The records name six wives of the men
who guarded the harem gate, who carried

97
00:07:16,284 --> 00:07:20,392
messages between the women
inside and their allies outside.

98
00:07:20,942 --> 00:07:26,269
One of the women inside wrote to her
brother, a commander of archers in Nubia.

99
00:07:26,819 --> 00:07:32,408
Her message, as recorded at the trial,
was blunt: incite the people, make enmity,

100
00:07:32,408 --> 00:07:35,513
and come to make rebellion
against your lord.

101
00:07:36,063 --> 00:07:38,277
This was not just a murder plot.

102
00:07:42,427 --> 00:07:48,249
It was meant to be a coup, with an uprising
outside the palace to put Pentawere on

103
00:07:48,249 --> 00:07:49,030
the throne.

104
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:56,093
And the conspirators were
not only relying on knives.

105
00:07:56,643 --> 00:08:02,722
Other papyri describe how one of the accused,
an official named Penhuibin, obtained

106
00:08:02,722 --> 00:08:05,579
a book of magic from the royal library.

107
00:08:06,129 --> 00:08:11,463
With it, he made figures of wax and wrote
magical spells, intended to weaken the

108
00:08:11,463 --> 00:08:14,863
guards and paralyze the
people who might stop them.

109
00:08:15,413 --> 00:08:19,038
To the ancient Egyptians,
this was not superstition.

110
00:08:19,588 --> 00:08:25,273
It was a weapon, and the court treated it
as a crime as serious as the plot itself.

111
00:08:25,823 --> 00:08:28,034
There is a dark irony here.

112
00:08:28,584 --> 00:08:32,479
The Egyptian state itself
used magic as a weapon.

113
00:08:33,029 --> 00:08:38,567
For centuries, priests had made figures
of Egypt's enemies, written their names on

114
00:08:38,567 --> 00:08:43,429
them, and broken or buried them in
rituals meant to destroy their power.

115
00:08:43,979 --> 00:08:49,515
Now that same kind of magic was being
turned against the king, from inside his own

116
00:08:49,515 --> 00:08:52,283
house, using a book from his own library.

117
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:56,219
There is one more strange
detail in the records.

118
00:08:56,769 --> 00:09:00,668
Many of the names of the accused
are not their real names.

119
00:09:01,218 --> 00:09:03,971
The scribes changed them into insults.

120
00:09:04,521 --> 00:09:07,371
Mesedsure means Ra hates him.

121
00:09:07,921 --> 00:09:11,057
Binemwese means evil in Thebes.

122
00:09:11,607 --> 00:09:17,184
Even in the court records, the conspirators
were punished by having their true names

123
00:09:17,184 --> 00:09:17,649
erased.

124
00:09:26,199 --> 00:09:32,210
Sometime in the thirty second year
of his reign, around 1155 B.C.E., the

125
00:09:32,210 --> 00:09:33,880
conspirators struck.

126
00:09:35,430 --> 00:09:40,932
We do not know exactly where, or how many
people were involved in the attack itself.

127
00:09:41,482 --> 00:09:44,183
The records do not describe the night.

128
00:09:44,733 --> 00:09:50,067
Some historians have suggested that it
may have happened at Medinet Habu itself,

129
00:09:50,067 --> 00:09:55,334
where a royal palace stood right beside
the temple, connected to it by a window

130
00:09:55,334 --> 00:09:58,134
where the king appeared before his people.

131
00:09:58,684 --> 00:10:03,865
It would have been a place where the king
lived close to the women of his household,

132
00:10:03,865 --> 00:10:06,888
and where those who served
him could come and go.

133
00:10:07,438 --> 00:10:10,284
But this is only a reasonable guess.

134
00:10:10,834 --> 00:10:12,556
But we know the result.

135
00:10:13,106 --> 00:10:15,397
Ramesses the Third died.

136
00:10:15,947 --> 00:10:18,221
And then the plan fell apart.

137
00:10:18,771 --> 00:10:21,057
The uprising never happened.

138
00:10:21,607 --> 00:10:25,362
The crown prince, Ramesses
the Fourth, took the throne.

139
00:10:25,912 --> 00:10:29,189
And the conspirators, one
by one, were arrested.

140
00:10:34,739 --> 00:10:40,306
The trial that followed is one of the most
remarkable legal records from the ancient

141
00:10:40,306 --> 00:10:40,704
world.

142
00:10:41,254 --> 00:10:46,067
The papyrus is written as if the dead
king himself were speaking, giving

143
00:10:46,067 --> 00:10:47,805
instructions to the court.

144
00:10:48,355 --> 00:10:54,117
He appoints a panel of officials, more
than a dozen of them, treasurers, butlers,

145
00:10:54,117 --> 00:10:57,673
scribes and army officers,
to examine the accused.

146
00:10:58,223 --> 00:11:01,384
These were not priests
or professional judges.

147
00:11:01,934 --> 00:11:07,119
They were senior servants of the crown,
men the king trusted, chosen to judge the

148
00:11:07,119 --> 00:11:08,912
people who had betrayed him.

149
00:11:09,462 --> 00:11:15,002
He tells them to punish those who are
guilty, and he insists that he himself knows

150
00:11:15,002 --> 00:11:18,920
nothing of the details, that
the responsibility is theirs.

151
00:11:19,470 --> 00:11:21,893
Then the record lists the verdicts.

152
00:11:22,443 --> 00:11:27,788
The accused are divided into groups, depending
on their crimes and their punishment.

153
00:11:28,338 --> 00:11:33,498
The first and largest group were brought
before the court, examined, and found

154
00:11:33,498 --> 00:11:38,194
guilty, and their punishment, the
papyrus says simply, was carried out.

155
00:11:38,744 --> 00:11:44,207
Others were allowed to die by their own
hand, a privilege of rank that spared them

156
00:11:44,207 --> 00:11:49,071
the public execution and, perhaps,
gave them some hope for the afterlife.

157
00:11:49,621 --> 00:11:54,217
Group after group of defendants,
each with the same grim formula.

158
00:11:54,767 --> 00:11:58,900
Pebekkamen, the chief of the
chamber, was found guilty.

159
00:11:59,450 --> 00:12:01,462
So was Mesedsure.

160
00:12:02,012 --> 00:12:07,403
So were the overseers, the scribes, the
butlers, and the commander of archers.

161
00:12:07,953 --> 00:12:09,649
Many were executed.

162
00:12:10,199 --> 00:12:14,020
Others, of higher rank, were
allowed to take their own lives.

163
00:12:14,570 --> 00:12:19,246
Among them was Pentawere himself,
the prince who would have been king.

164
00:12:19,796 --> 00:12:21,001
And Tiye?

165
00:12:21,551 --> 00:12:25,342
Strangely, the records never
tell us what happened to her.

166
00:12:28,892 --> 00:12:31,810
But then the trial took a scandalous turn.

167
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:37,565
Some of the judges were caught partying
with the very women they were supposed to be

168
00:12:37,565 --> 00:12:38,061
judging.

169
00:12:38,611 --> 00:12:41,044
They were put on trial themselves.

170
00:12:41,594 --> 00:12:47,282
Their punishment, according to the papyrus,
was to have their noses and ears cut

171
00:12:47,282 --> 00:12:47,566
off.

172
00:12:48,116 --> 00:12:51,421
One of them took his own
life rather than face it.

173
00:12:55,571 --> 00:13:00,869
Even the court that judged the murder of
the king was not immune to corruption.

174
00:13:06,419 --> 00:13:12,613
For more than a century after the papyrus
was translated, historians argued about

175
00:13:12,613 --> 00:13:13,607
one question.

176
00:13:14,157 --> 00:13:17,061
Did the conspirators
actually kill the king?

177
00:13:17,611 --> 00:13:21,150
The trial records are
strangely silent about it.

178
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:27,544
Some scholars believed Ramesses survived
the attack and died later, perhaps of his

179
00:13:27,544 --> 00:13:29,468
wounds, perhaps of old age.

180
00:13:30,018 --> 00:13:35,614
The answer lay in a hidden tomb in the
cliffs of Deir el-Bahari, near Thebes.

181
00:13:39,164 --> 00:13:45,492
In 1881, Egyptian authorities learned of
a secret burial that had been looted for

182
00:13:45,492 --> 00:13:47,367
years by a local family.

183
00:13:47,917 --> 00:13:53,523
Inside were the mummies of dozens of
pharaohs and royal relatives, moved there by

184
00:13:53,523 --> 00:13:58,644
priests around three thousand years
ago to protect them from tomb robbers.

185
00:13:59,194 --> 00:14:05,000
For a decade, royal antiquities had been
appearing on the antiquities market, and

186
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:10,233
the authorities finally traced them
to the Abd el-Rassul family of Gurna.

187
00:14:10,783 --> 00:14:15,531
Under pressure, one of the brothers
led officials to a shaft in the cliffs.

188
00:14:16,081 --> 00:14:21,544
The museum official Émile Brugsch was
lowered into the tomb, and found himself

189
00:14:21,544 --> 00:14:23,996
surrounded by the coffins of kings.

190
00:14:24,546 --> 00:14:30,179
In just a few days, the mummies were carried
out and taken by boat down the Nile to

191
00:14:30,179 --> 00:14:30,586
Cairo.

192
00:14:31,136 --> 00:14:33,961
Among them was Ramesses the Third.

193
00:14:37,511 --> 00:14:43,820
In 1886, the head of the antiquities
service, Gaston Maspero, unwrapped the king's

194
00:14:43,820 --> 00:14:46,820
mummy in front of an audience in Cairo.

195
00:14:47,370 --> 00:14:52,834
But the linen around the neck was thick,
hardened with resin, and Maspero left it in

196
00:14:52,834 --> 00:14:53,224
place.

197
00:14:53,774 --> 00:14:59,120
That decision kept the king's secret
hidden for another hundred and twenty six

198
00:14:59,120 --> 00:14:59,531
years.

199
00:15:00,081 --> 00:15:05,520
And beside the kings was something very
strange: the body of a young man, buried

200
00:15:05,520 --> 00:15:06,540
without a name.

201
00:15:07,090 --> 00:15:09,868
His mummy had not been properly prepared.

202
00:15:10,418 --> 00:15:15,901
He had been wrapped in a goatskin, a
material that was considered ritually impure.

203
00:15:16,451 --> 00:15:21,528
His face was frozen in an expression
that earned him a nickname: the screaming

204
00:15:21,528 --> 00:15:21,918
mummy.

205
00:15:22,468 --> 00:15:25,718
For more than a century,
no one knew who he was.

206
00:15:26,268 --> 00:15:27,758
Theories piled up.

207
00:15:28,308 --> 00:15:33,782
Some suggested he had been poisoned, others
that he had been buried alive, others

208
00:15:33,782 --> 00:15:37,228
that he was a foreign prince
who had died in Egypt.

209
00:15:43,778 --> 00:15:50,415
In 2012, a team led by Egyptologist
Zahi Hawass and radiologist Sahar Saleem

210
00:15:50,415 --> 00:15:54,781
published the results of
CT scans of both mummies.

211
00:15:55,331 --> 00:16:00,861
The scan of Ramesses the Third showed a
deep wound across his throat, about seven

212
00:16:00,861 --> 00:16:04,685
centimeters wide, cutting
down to the bones of the neck.

213
00:16:05,235 --> 00:16:08,670
It severed the windpipe and
the major blood vessels.

214
00:16:09,220 --> 00:16:11,518
Death would have been almost immediate.

215
00:16:12,068 --> 00:16:17,220
The embalmers had placed an amulet, an
Eye of Horus, a symbol of healing and

216
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:22,507
protection, directly into the wound, and
then wrapped the neck in thick linen.

217
00:16:23,058 --> 00:16:26,683
Later study of the scans
found a second injury.

218
00:16:27,233 --> 00:16:32,077
One of the king's big toes had been
cut off, probably by a heavy blade.

219
00:16:32,627 --> 00:16:35,154
The bone showed no signs of healing.

220
00:16:39,304 --> 00:16:44,599
Taken together, the injuries suggest that
the king was attacked by more than one

221
00:16:44,599 --> 00:16:48,040
person, from different
directions, at the same time.

222
00:16:48,590 --> 00:16:50,159
The verdict was in.

223
00:16:50,709 --> 00:16:53,543
The plot to kill the king had succeeded.

224
00:16:57,093 --> 00:16:58,681
And the screaming mummy?

225
00:16:59,231 --> 00:17:05,564
DNA analysis showed that the young man
shared his paternal lineage with Ramesses the

226
00:17:05,564 --> 00:17:06,016
Third.

227
00:17:06,566 --> 00:17:08,245
He was the king's son.

228
00:17:08,795 --> 00:17:14,018
His age, his dishonorable burial, and
the goatskin all point to one person.

229
00:17:14,568 --> 00:17:20,276
Many researchers believe that the screaming
mummy is Pentawere, the prince who was

230
00:17:20,276 --> 00:17:25,219
allowed to take his own life, and
who was denied a proper royal burial.

231
00:17:25,769 --> 00:17:27,846
We cannot be completely certain.

232
00:17:28,396 --> 00:17:30,662
But it is the best match we have.

233
00:17:39,812 --> 00:17:43,462
In the end, the conspiracy
both failed and succeeded.

234
00:17:44,012 --> 00:17:47,820
The king was dead, but the
conspirators never took power.

235
00:17:48,370 --> 00:17:54,290
Ramesses the Fourth ruled, and Egypt
continued, though its golden age was over.

236
00:17:54,840 --> 00:18:00,042
At Abydos, the new king set up a
stela with a prayer to the god Osiris.

237
00:18:00,592 --> 00:18:06,627
He asked for a long life and a long reign,
longer even than the sixty seven years of

238
00:18:06,627 --> 00:18:07,992
Ramesses the Great.

239
00:18:08,542 --> 00:18:10,694
He reigned for about six years.

240
00:18:11,244 --> 00:18:17,572
His murdered father was buried in the Valley
of the Kings, in a long tomb now called

241
00:18:17,572 --> 00:18:17,949
KV11.

242
00:18:18,499 --> 00:18:23,855
Travelers later named it the Tomb of the
Harpers, after a painting of two blind

243
00:18:23,855 --> 00:18:26,364
musicians playing harps for the gods.

244
00:18:26,914 --> 00:18:32,875
Today the granite box of his sarcophagus
sits in the Louvre in Paris, and its lid is

245
00:18:32,875 --> 00:18:35,642
in the Fitzwilliam Museum in Cambridge.

246
00:18:36,192 --> 00:18:41,958
The king himself was taken from his tomb
by priests centuries later, rewrapped, and

247
00:18:41,958 --> 00:18:47,794
moved from hiding place to hiding place,
until he came to rest in the royal cache at

248
00:18:47,794 --> 00:18:48,836
Deir el-Bahari.

249
00:18:49,386 --> 00:18:53,084
Within a century, the New
Kingdom would come to an end.

250
00:18:53,634 --> 00:18:58,509
In fact, Egypt would soon need more
courts like the one that judged the

251
00:18:58,509 --> 00:18:59,402
conspirators.

252
00:18:59,952 --> 00:19:05,770
Under Ramesses the Ninth, officials
investigated a wave of tomb robberies in the

253
00:19:05,770 --> 00:19:11,733
royal necropolis, and their reports, now
known as the tomb robbery papyri, survive

254
00:19:11,733 --> 00:19:12,024
too.

255
00:19:12,574 --> 00:19:17,722
They tell of thieves breaking into royal
tombs, stripping the gold from kings, and

256
00:19:17,722 --> 00:19:21,301
sharing the loot with officials
who looked the other way.

257
00:19:21,851 --> 00:19:27,223
The tombs in the Valley of the Kings would
be looted, and the priests would hide the

258
00:19:27,223 --> 00:19:32,467
royal mummies away, where they would wait
three thousand years for someone to read

259
00:19:32,467 --> 00:19:34,897
the evidence written into their bones.

260
00:19:38,447 --> 00:19:41,364
A king murdered by his own household.

261
00:19:41,914 --> 00:19:44,743
A trial corrupted by its own judges.

262
00:19:45,293 --> 00:19:51,054
And a cold case solved by a scanner, more
than three thousand years after the crime.

263
00:19:58,604 --> 00:20:01,580
Thank you for watching
Ancient World Files.

